e('SSDs also cache writes in volatile DRAM. Enterprise drives have **power-loss protection** (capacitors that hold enough charge to flush), so they can acknowledge a flush quickly and safely. Consumer drives often don\'t, which is why `fsync` on them is slower, and why a drive that ignores flush commands can lose acknowledged writes, as the crash consistency lesson warned.','Some SSDs buffer data or mapping metadata in volatile memory. Power-loss protection can preserve specified state, but its coverage and presence vary. A compliant unprotected device can still honor flushes by persisting data before completion; one that dishonors the durability contract can lose acknowledged data. No universal enterprise/consumer fsync-speed ordering is implied.','PLPnotall/noPLPcanflushsafe.')
e('| Queues | 1 | up to 65,535 |','| I/O queues | one command list per AHCI port | protocol space for up to 65,535 I/O queues |','AHCI perport;NVMeIO notadmin.',nv)
