e('the rebuild must read **every sector of every surviving disk**.','a conventional full-device rebuild reconstructs the failed member by reading the corresponding allocated array stripes on surviving members. Optimized/partial rebuilds and filesystem-aware layouts can skip unused or known-clean ranges.','Not everydeviceeverysector.',rr)
e('Drive data sheets quote an **unrecoverable read error (URE)** rate, commonly "less than 1 in 10¹⁴ bits" for desktop drives and 1 in 10¹⁵ for enterprise drives. 10¹⁴ bits is 12.5 TB.','Device specifications can state nonrecoverable-read-error limits over bits read. Such a limit is not, by itself, an observed independent per-bit probability model. For unit conversion,10¹⁴ bits equals12.5 TB.','Spec !=stochastic observedrate.',rr)
e('At the spec-sheet worst case of 1 URE per 10¹⁴ bits, the expected number of errors is 3.84, and the chance of zero errors is about e^−3.84 ≈ **2%**.','If, purely for a mathematical scenario, errors follow an independent Poisson model with mean rate1 per10¹⁴ bits, the expected count is3.84 and P(zero)=e^−3.84≈**2.15%**. A datasheet limit alone does not justify this distribution.','Explicitmodelnotriskprediction.',rr)
e('Real drives usually do much better than their worst-case spec, so this overstates the risk. But the direction is right, and the rebuild also takes a day or more at ~150 MB/s, during which a second whole-disk failure kills the array.','No empirical rebuild-failure probability follows without measured error behavior and rebuild details. At150 MB/s per parallel survivor/replacement stream,16 TB takes about29.6 hours before overhead; treating150 MB/s as aggregate48 TB read bandwidth instead gives about88.9 hours. Another complete member failure exceeds RAID5 redundancy, while a URE can affect a stripe or abort recovery depending on implementation.','Notclaimrealusually/ambiguousbandwidth/wholearrayURE.',rr)
e('**Drives from the same batch fail together.** They share age, firmware and workload, so a second failure during rebuild is likelier than independent maths suggests.','**Correlated failures matter.** Shared environment, age, firmware and workload can invalidate independence assumptions; this does not establish that a particular batch will fail together.','Not certainbatchfailure.',rr)
e('If any of 4 disks fails, everything is lost, so the array fails roughly four times as often as one disk.','A member failure can make a striped RAID0 volume unusable even though some surviving sectors remain readable. With independent equal small failure probability p over a chosen interval, array failure probability is1−(1−p)^4≈4p; correlation and repair alter reliability models.','Allbytesgone/4xrequiresindependence.',rr)
e('- HDDs pay milliseconds of seek and rotation per random I/O (about 80–200 IOPS) but stream sequentially at hundreds of MB/s, which is why file systems work so hard at locality.','- HDD seek and rotation make access locality important; use identified device/workload measurements rather than a universal IOPS range.','Summaryunmeasured.',hd)
e('NVMe replaces AHCI\'s single 32-command queue with many deep per-core queues over PCIe, cutting latency to tens of microseconds and making software overhead visible.','NVMe supports multiple deep I/O queues; actual CPU-to-queue mapping and latency depend on implementation and workload.','Summaryprotocolnotguaranteedlatency.',nv)
