f(6,'prompt','Which actions can provide the FTL with more unused logical capacity and help reduce GC write amplification, assuming a workload/device that can benefit? Select all that apply.','No guaranteedallactionWA.')
f(6,'text','Leave20% of LBAs never written, or successfully discard them before keeping that space unused','DeletingpartitionnotTRIM.',o=1)
f(6,'explanation','This can provide effective spare capacity when the FTL knows those LBAs are unused; partition-table changes alone need not invalidate old data.','Overprovisioncondition.',o=1)
f(6,'explanation','Routine defragmentation adds host writes and is not the listed way to expose unused LBAs. Layout changes can still help some flash workloads; extra host writes are not automatically the same as a larger flash/host WA ratio.','No benefit/WAhostdenominatorfalse.',o=2)
f(6,'workedExample','TRIM/discard can identify no-longer-used LBAs; never-written or successfully discarded unused capacity can act as spare space. Effects depend on firmware and workload. Keeping nearly all logical space live can constrain GC. Routine defragmentation adds writes and does not automatically reduce the live set; it is not universally useless, nor does more host traffic itself prove an increased WA ratio.','NuanceWAnottraffic.')
