f(9,'prompt','A six-disk RAID5 of12TB members loses one disk. Assume a full rebuild reads60TB from survivors and, as a hypothetical mathematical model, independent URE events form a Poisson process at mean rate1 per10^15 bits. What is P(at least one URE)?','Datasheetlimitnotprobabilitymodel.',rr)
f(9,'explanation','The question supplies a hypothetical mean rate, not a claim that an enterprise label prevents errors or that a datasheet bound alone defines a probability distribution.','Not specisprobability.',rr,o=3)
f(9,'workedExample','The assumed read volume is5×12TB=60×10^12 bytes=4.8×10^14 bits. Under the explicitly supplied Poisson model, lambda=0.48, so P(at least one)=1−exp(−0.48)≈38.12%. This is not an empirical failure forecast derived solely from a vendor error-rate limit. RAID6 can tolerate one missing disk plus one unreadable chunk in a stripe, but multiple additional errors or other faults can exceed redundancy.','Poissonnotrealrisk/RAID6notallURErecoverable.',rr)
