f(12,'prompt','During a hypothetical direct-I/O SSD random-write benchmark, telemetry rules out thermal throttling and SLC-cache exhaustion but shows free-block pressure and foreground GC. Throughput falls and tails increase. Which mechanism explains this?','SymptomcouldSLC/thermal/notassumeODIRECT.')
f(12,'text','Free-block pressure makes GC compete with host writes to copy live pages and erase blocks, adding traffic and possible stalls','Noteverywritewaits.',o=1)
f(12,'explanation','Correct under the supplied telemetry. Background/foreground GC and internal parallelism determine which requests wait.','Noalleverywrite.',o=1)
f(12,'explanation','Thermal throttling is a plausible cause of slowdown in general; here it has explicitly been ruled out rather than inferred absent from timing.','Cannotdiagnosejustpattern.',o=0)
f(12,'explanation','The question explicitly specifies a direct-I/O path and GC telemetry. An unspecified benchmark could instead be limited by host dirty-page throttling.','Not allgoodbenchODIRECT.',o=2)
f(12,'workedExample','The specified telemetry identifies GC contention: valid pages are moved before erase creates reusable blocks, consuming bandwidth and potentially delaying requests. SLC-cache folding, thermal throttling, host buffering and other causes must normally be distinguished. A throughput drop alone does not establish steady state or a particular WA ratio. Follow a documented preconditioning and stability criterion such as the SNIA test specification; do not assume that a fixed few-minute run or one fill reaches steady state.','Noallfactoryerased/3-5unmeasured/steady definition.',pts)
